WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.48
This is journey twenty two of Hewn, the software factory: connect a GitHub

00:00:06.48 --> 00:00:07.22
repository.

00:00:07.22 --> 00:00:10.44
Every Hewn project lives in a GitHub repository.

00:00:10.44 --> 00:00:13.40
An Owner either names one they already have,

00:00:13.40 --> 00:00:16.43
or leaves the field empty and Hewn makes one.

00:00:16.43 --> 00:00:21.34
This video walks through how that works: checking an existing repository,

00:00:21.34 --> 00:00:24.97
creating a private one, protecting its default branch,

00:00:24.97 --> 00:00:28.93
and how every token Hewn uses is limited to one repository.

00:00:30.08 --> 00:00:34.97
An existing repository must be a GitHub URL that Hewn can write to,

00:00:34.97 --> 00:00:38.25
or nothing is created and the error says why.

00:00:38.25 --> 00:00:43.86
With no repository, stage 0 creates a private one under the configured owner,

00:00:43.86 --> 00:00:46.56
named from the project in kebab case.

00:00:46.56 --> 00:00:50.71
After stage 6, the default branch requires the CI checks.

00:00:50.71 --> 00:00:56.25
And every call to GitHub uses an installation token limited to the project's

00:00:56.25 --> 00:00:57.05
repository.

00:00:58.20 --> 00:01:03.42
When the Owner submits, the URL is checked first: it must be h t t p s,

00:01:03.42 --> 00:01:05.48
github dot com, owner, repo.

00:01:05.48 --> 00:01:09.96
Then Hewn asks GitHub, as its App, whether it can push there.

00:01:09.96 --> 00:01:15.40
A repository it can't find, or can't write to, is refused with the reason,

00:01:15.40 --> 00:01:16.87
on the field itself.

00:01:17.22 --> 00:01:21.53
Here the Owner names a repository the App isn't installed on.

00:01:21.53 --> 00:01:24.07
The error says so and how to fix it.

00:01:24.07 --> 00:01:29.87
With one Hewn can write to, the project starts, and its page links the repository.

00:01:31.02 --> 00:01:34.11
Here is the flow when there is no repository.

00:01:34.11 --> 00:01:39.54
When a worker first leases stage 0, the provisioner turns the project name into

00:01:39.54 --> 00:01:41.39
kebab case and looks it up.

00:01:41.39 --> 00:01:45.45
If it's absent, Hewn creates it, private, and tags it hewn,

00:01:45.45 --> 00:01:49.50
so a crashed attempt can be adopted rather than duplicated.

00:01:49.50 --> 00:01:55.00
Then stage 6 merges the scaffold and asks the API to protect the default branch.

00:01:55.35 --> 00:01:59.19
Here, Kiln Scheduler is submitted with no repository.

00:01:59.19 --> 00:02:03.90
Moments later, stage 0 has made hewn builds slash kiln scheduler,

00:02:03.90 --> 00:02:06.65
and the link appears without a reload.

00:02:07.00 --> 00:02:09.52
The fake GitHub keeps the record.

00:02:09.52 --> 00:02:14.48
The repository is private, tagged hewn, and once stage 6 has run,

00:02:14.48 --> 00:02:19.44
its default branch requires the backend, frontend and e2e checks.

00:02:20.59 --> 00:02:23.79
This is the change journey twenty two made.

00:02:23.79 --> 00:02:30.05
Every call for a project's repository exchanges the App's key for a token that names

00:02:30.05 --> 00:02:31.53
that one repository.

00:02:31.88 --> 00:02:34.84
Creating a repository can't name it yet.

00:02:34.84 --> 00:02:38.83
That token used to carry every permission the App has,

00:02:38.83 --> 00:02:41.26
on every repository it can reach.

00:02:41.26 --> 00:02:44.51
Now it asks only to administer repositories.

00:02:44.86 --> 00:02:46.71
And it's used for one call.

00:02:46.71 --> 00:02:52.19
Straight after creating the repository, Hewn asks for a token limited to the new

00:02:52.19 --> 00:02:54.52
repository, and tags it with that.

00:02:54.52 --> 00:02:58.77
A d r twenty four records this, with the App's permission set.

00:02:59.92 --> 00:03:04.81
Branch protection is a request the worker makes once the scaffold merges.

00:03:04.81 --> 00:03:09.24
The handler checks the worker's lease, refuses it outside stage 6,

00:03:09.24 --> 00:03:12.79
and sets the required checks with a repository token.

00:03:13.94 --> 00:03:18.46
The new tests record every token exchange against a stub GitHub.

00:03:18.46 --> 00:03:23.13
Creating a repository failed first: its token had no scope at all.

00:03:23.13 --> 00:03:28.93
Now three pass, and the backend has two hundred and seventy nine tests, all green.

00:03:29.28 --> 00:03:34.85
End to end, the run pipeline test now reads the fake GitHub's record: a private

00:03:34.85 --> 00:03:38.59
repository from stage 0, and, once the run completes,

00:03:38.59 --> 00:03:42.32
the scaffold's checks required on the default branch.

00:03:42.32 --> 00:03:47.75
The checks on existing repositories were already covered by earlier journeys,

00:03:47.75 --> 00:03:48.80
and still pass.

00:03:49.95 --> 00:03:54.20
The repository error reads the same in dark mode, and on a phone.

00:03:55.35 --> 00:03:57.58
Journey twenty two is complete.

00:03:57.58 --> 00:04:01.39
An existing repository must be one Hewn can write to.

00:04:01.39 --> 00:04:06.06
Without one, stage 0 creates it private, and stage 6 protects it.

00:04:06.06 --> 00:04:09.72
Every token is limited to the project's repository,

00:04:09.72 --> 00:04:13.89
and the one that creates repositories can do nothing else.

00:04:13.89 --> 00:04:16.19
Next comes journey twenty three.
